  <page>
  <definition xml:id="poisson">
    <statement>
      <p>
         If <m>X</m> has pmf <fillin characters="25"/> then the RV <m>X</m> is said
      to
    </p>

    <p>
      have a <fillin characters="25"/> distribution.
    </p>
      
    </statement>
    </definition>
      
    <exercise workspace = "1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Applying this to our phone call example, then what is
      <m>\lambda</m>? <m>P(X=25)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
 

    <p>
      In general, Poisson processes applies to events that occur rather
      uniformly, but rather infrequently per small unit of time or space. Some examples include:
    </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            number of accidents during some time period
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            number of flaws in an industrial process
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            number of claims at an insurance company
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            number of fish caught during a fishing trip
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            number of calls coming in on a hotline
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            number of customers lining up in at checkout
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            emissions of radioactive particles over a fixed period of time...
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
   
