</page>
<page>
    <p>
      <alert>Summary: How to solve a Poisson Problem</alert>
    </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Identify what a <q>change</q> is for your situation (<q> success</q>, etc.)
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Identify the length of your interval of interest (e.g. 4 minutes, 5
            yards, etc.)
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Determine how many <q>change's</q> you expect during your interval.
            This is given to you in the problem, but you may need to adjust it
            for your interval length. For example it might tell you how many
            changes expected per minute, but you are interested in changes over
            a 5 minute period so you would multiply by 5. This value is
            <m>\lambda</m>. (Note: <m>\lambda</m> does not have to be a whole
            number)
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Write your problem as <m>P(X=k), P(X \leq k),</m> or
            <m>1-P(X\leq k)</m> and solve.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose flaws occur in a piece of sheet metal on average of
            <m>15</m> per linear yard.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace = "1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the probability that in a given yard, there are <m>20</m>
                  flaws.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace ="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the probability of at most <m>3</m> flaws in a
                  <m>0.5</m> linear yard?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
   </exercise>  
  <exercise> 
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose that customers line up to buy tickets to a show on average
            of <m>5</m> every <m>10</m> minutes.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assuming a Poisson process, what is the probability that there
                  will be <m>40</m> customers in an hour?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the probability of at least <m>20</m> customers in a
                  half hour?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
   </exercise>
</page>
<page>
          <p>
            We started by showing that the binomial distribution was used to
            derive the Poisson distribution, but it turns out the Poisson can
            actually be used to approximate the binomial distribution. If
            <m>X \sim b(n,p)</m> with <m>n</m> <q>large</q> and <m>p</m> <q>small</q>,
            then the Poisson approximates <m>X</m> using
            <fillin characters="25"/>.The rule of thumb is to only
            use this if <m>n\geq 20</m> and <m>p \leq 0.05</m> or
            <m>n \geq 100</m> and <m>p \leq 0.10</m>.
          </p>

          <image width="80%" source="PoissonBinomial.PNG"/>
